+## Self-check
+
+- **The regression test FAILS with the product change reverted and PASSES with
+  it (exit codes).** RED: `git apply -R fix.patch` → build exit **2**
+  (`build-11-qt5-reversal-red.log`), ctest exit **8** (`test-4-qt5-reversal-red.log`,
+  `Not Run`). GREEN: `git apply fix.patch` → build exit **0**
+  (`build-12-qt5-reversal-green.log`), ctest exit **0**
+  (`test-5-qt5-reversal-green.log`, 1/1). The test binary was deleted before the
+  RED build so a stale binary could not launder the result.
+- **The test is deterministic: no wall-clock timing, sleeps or races.** The new
+  slot writes three files of a fixed size and compares their digests with
+  `sha256sum`-computed constants. The only seam is the `data` search path, which
+  the slot sets and restores around one `script.list` call, exactly like the
+  neighbouring slots. No clocks, no threads, no sleeps.
+- **The fix is at the cause and complete.** The reported site
+  (`ControlCommandsScript.cpp:30` and `:106`) and its test-side twin
+  (`ControlAutomationScriptTest.cpp:34` and `:574`) are both guarded with the
+  same `QT_VERSION` pattern the sibling files use. Every `QByteArrayView` in the
+  tree was re-grepped: only the two guarded sites and the two pre-existing
+  guarded sites remain. The other three Qt 5 failures were found by building, not
+  by reading, and each was fixed at its own cause.
+- **No collateral damage.** The Qt 5 `QByteArray` overload and the Qt 6
+  `QByteArrayView` overload hash the same bytes; the boundary test proves the
+  digests are the `sha256sum` values of the file contents on both toolchains.
+  `OscBridge` still decodes 8 bytes with `quint64`; `TelemetryConsentDialog`
+  only gained an include. No limit or check was added, so no other caller can be
+  newly refused.
+- **Sibling commands.** This commit touches exactly one command file,
+  `src/core/ControlCommandsScript.cpp`, whose group is `script.*`: `script.list`
+  (read-only, uses the fixed hasher), `script.run` and `script.set_memory_budget`
+  (mutating, do not touch the hasher). All three are unchanged in behaviour; no
+  new command was added, so `ControlCommandsSnapshot` is untouched. The
+  pre-submit check's longer sibling list (`app.*`, `arrangement.*`) comes from
+  other files changed on the train between the base and HEAD, not from this
+  commit; none of them is touched here and none calls the script hasher.
+- **Realtime.** Nothing on an audio-thread path changed. The hasher runs in the
+  `script.list` command handler; `OscBridge` is the OSC command bridge; the
+  telemetry dialog is a GUI dialog. No allocation, lock, wait or log was added to
+  `processImpl`/`playImpl`/mixer/NotePlayHandle paths.
+- **Registration.** No new source or test file was added, so
+  `tests/fork-sources.txt` and `tests/upstream-modifications.txt` are unchanged.
+  `include/TelemetryConsentDialog.h`, `src/core/OscBridge.cpp` and
+  `src/core/ControlCommandsScript.cpp` are already registered fork sources;
+  `tests/src/core/ControlAutomationScriptTest.cpp` is already in
+  `upstream-modifications.txt`.
+- **Portability paths.** Qt 5.15.19 (this box) and Qt 6.12 (this box) both build
+  the affected targets and pass the test. The hosted Qt 5 job is the one this
+  fixes. MSVC/macOS/mingw were not run here; no platform-specific API was added.
